from pathlib import Path
import re, json, hashlib
from docx import Document
from docx.text.paragraph import Paragraph
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
from docx.oxml.ns import qn

root = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
source = root / 'GEC1044_Tong_Keow_Readability_Revision_v2.docx'
output = root / 'GEC1044_Tong_Keow_Argument_Revision.docx'
d = Document(source)
updates = {
6: 'This revision builds the argument around everyday care, convenience and localisation, with the seven survey records retained in Appendix A. Each theme connects the fieldwork to its significance for Tong Keow’s retail setting. Staff accounts, photographs and our interpretations remain distinct, and industry examples are identified as promotional, journalistic or public-relations sources. Section 6 uses the ginseng profiles to explain why individual assessment matters. Repeated qualifications are consolidated in the limitations section.',
8: 'At Tong Keow’s Hougang outlet, dried herbs and packaged soups sit alongside grains and other everyday foods. A staff member described customers buying products for nourishment, throat comfort, fatigue and concerns about their daily routines. The shelves and interview together place Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) within ordinary shopping and cooking. This everyday setting is the starting point of our report.',
9: 'We argue that Tong Keow helps sustain TCM by fitting traditional materials into familiar household practices. Soup ingredients give herbs a place in meals; prepared packs reduce the work of using them; local branding and manufacture connect them to Singapore’s retail setting. These adaptations offer practical ways to encounter TCM. Our analysis follows these connections and then considers the individual assessment involved in clinical treatment. Appendix A contains the detailed survey findings.',
13: 'Throughout the report, staff descriptions refer to the interview and descriptions of displays refer to our photographs. We identify our interpretations when connecting these findings to the argument. Published sources provide historical, demographic and industry context. Statements about popular and lower-demand products represent the staff member’s retail experience.',
16: 'The tutor described Tong Keow as a chain, and the directory links its Toa Payoh and Hougang outlets. At Hougang, we photographed Tong Keow branding on soup packs alongside herbs, grains and other foods. The branded packs connect the branch to the wider business, while the surrounding food range places medicinal and culinary shopping in the same space. We interpret this combination as central to the outlet’s everyday role: customers can encounter herbal goods during an ordinary food-shopping trip.',
19: 'Hougang Central, the smaller subzone around the outlet, had about 4,620 residents in June 2025. Using the official rounded age-band totals, around 1,070 were aged 65 or above, roughly 23% of the population (DOS, 2025). This substantial older population gives a local setting for the middle-aged and older clientele described by staff. The shop’s household foods and nourishment products can be understood within this residential context.',
20: 'Chua et al. (2025) add a picture of long-term neighbourhood life. Their 647 Hougang respondents had an average age of 69 and had lived in the area for an average of 22 years. Seventeen per cent lived alone, compared with 15% of 552 respondents in Taman Jurong; that difference was not statistically significant. For our analysis, the long residence is especially relevant: it places the medicine hall within an established community where household shopping and care take place.',
21: 'Hougang’s history of Teochew settlement also forms part of this setting. The National Heritage Board records the community around Tou Mu Kung and its fishing, farming and rubber-growing activities (National Heritage Board [NHB], n.d.). We interpret the area’s Chinese community history and established residential population as context for the mixed medicinal and food range observed at Tong Keow. The interview supplies the account of the outlet’s current customers.',
25: 'Soup gives herbal materials a familiar household purpose. We interpret the display through nourishing life, 养生, and medicine and food sharing a source, 药食同源: ingredients become part of cooking and routine nourishment. Lim et al.’s (2005) survey of complementary and alternative medicine in multiracial Singapore identified health maintenance as a significant reason for use. Yang’s (2022) history likewise places Chinese medicine within changing community and institutional life. These sources help explain the significance of our display photographs: household food preparation is one setting through which people can encounter TCM.',
27: 'Our presentation organised the soup findings into five labels: Qing Bu Liang 清补凉, Dang Shen–Huang Qi soup 党参北芪补气汤, Yu Zhu–Huai Shan soup 玉竹淮山润燥汤, goji–red date soup 枸杞红枣养生汤 and Five Finger Fig soup 五指毛桃健脾汤. Table A4 records these labels and the named ingredients. Together, they show how the presentation linked familiar soup preparations to specific forms of everyday care.',
28: 'The staff account and presentation associated these soups with digestion, moistening and light nourishment, 清补. Light replenishment offers an everyday approach to care, alongside the stronger tonification described as 大补. The soup packs give this vocabulary a practical form: buyers encounter a named preparation that they can cook at home. References to the Spleen and Liver describe TCM functions and relationships (World Health Organization [WHO], 2022). We interpret the packs as a way to bring these ideas into ordinary meals.',
30: 'The staff member linked monk fruit’s popularity and lighter soups to Singapore’s environment. Meteorological Service Singapore describes high temperatures, abundant rainfall and high humidity throughout the year, with monsoon and inter-monsoon periods (Meteorological Service Singapore [MSS], n.d.). This gives climatic context to the presentation’s phrase “without four distinct seasons” and to the staff member’s explanation of local demand.',
31: 'The Suwen reading connects seasonal habits with maintaining health (Unschuld & Tessenow, 2011, pp. 45–57). We interpret the staff member’s discussion of lighter soups and cooling drinks as a local expression of this environmental reasoning. Singapore’s weather becomes part of how staff explain the appeal of particular preparations. The ingredient profiles add precision to that account: Yu Zhu, for example, is associated with moistening dryness.',
34: 'The staff member described younger customers as buying fewer raw herbs and preferring teas, supplements and simpler preparations. Table A2 retains the eight presentation categories, including ginseng tonics, herbal teas, soup packs, mood-support products and medicated oils. The interview directs attention to the form in which herbal goods are offered. In the staff member’s account, an easier preparation helps connect these goods to younger customers’ routines.',
35: 'The soup packs make the work of convenience visible. Ingredients have already been selected and portioned, so the household can begin with cooking. Staff explain the intended use, and the buyer completes the preparation at home. Capsules offer a format with less preparation, while the photographed multi-buy promotion presents soup packs through a familiar retail offer. We interpret convenience as a redistribution of effort between seller and household. Traditional materials remain available in forms that ask less selection and preparation of the buyer.',
37: 'Comparative industry examples show businesses deliberately changing how customers encounter herbal goods. Nanyang Polytechnic describes Heng Foh Tong’s redesign of herbal drinks for millennials (Nanyang Polytechnic [NYP], n.d.). Amacha markets TCM-inspired milk teas, and a founder interview reported more than 150,000 cups sold in eight months (Amacha, n.d.; Angelynn T., 2026). Teck Soon’s owner reported a 30% sales increase after moving herbs from drawers to storefront displays in 1982 (Chin, 2024). Eu Yan Sang also offers a halal product category (Eu Yan Sang, n.d.). These are promotional, journalistic or institutional public-relations accounts, not peer-reviewed studies. Their value here lies in the specific strategies they describe: redesigning drinks, improving visibility and addressing different customer groups. They give industry context to Tong Keow’s prepared packs and the staff account of convenience.',
39: 'The staff member associated late nights, long work hours, stress and prolonged phone use with liver-care or mood-support products. The supplied photograph illustrates this category with Nature’s Green Mind Soothe Capsules. Its packaging uses the TCM language of soothing Liver-related constraint, 疏肝解郁, alongside the English name “Mind Soothe”. We interpret this wording as a connection between traditional therapeutic vocabulary and familiar concerns about daily life. “Liver” carries the TCM meaning explained earlier.',
40: 'Broader labour data sharpen this reading. Average usual weekly working hours among employed residents fell from 44.2 a decade earlier to 41.4 in 2025 (Ministry of Manpower [MOM], 2026). The staff member’s account is therefore most useful as a description of perceived strain in customers’ routines. It shows how daily pressures enter retail advice; a claim that national working hours are increasing would conflict with the official figures.',
41: 'The lower-demand examples help explain the appeal of simpler formats. The presentation listed Ban Zhi Lian 半枝莲, specialist herbs used in combinations and strong tonic formulas 大补方 (Table A5). Staff linked their lower appeal to unfamiliarity and preparation effort, while the presentation discussed stronger tonics’ suitability in Singapore’s climate. Staff also said that poorly selling items were stocked in smaller quantities. Read alongside the prepared packs, these accounts suggest that the work of understanding and using a product matters to its place on the shelf.',
44: 'Localisation appears at several stages between a raw material and the product on the shelf. Staff described raw materials as coming mainly from China, citing variety and cost, with limited local cultivation. The presentation retained cactus 仙人掌, roselle 洛神花, patent medicines 中成药, Five Flower Tea 五花茶 and monk-fruit tea 罗汉果茶 as locally linked examples (Table A3). A specific manufacturing example is the pictured Nature’s Green Mind Soothe Capsules: the Health Sciences Authority (HSA) lists Tong Jum Chew as manufacturer and Singapore as the manufacturing country (HSA, 2025b). This places finished-medicine production within the local retail story.',
45: 'Tong Keow branding and preselected soup materials give herbs a recognisable retail form. Staff described local processing and packing. Our interpretation focuses on the visible work of selecting and portioning ingredients, naming their uses and presenting them under a local brand. This makes packaging an active part of localisation. Table A3 distinguishes cultivation, manufacture and packaging so that each example’s connection to Singapore remains clear.',
46: 'Singapore’s regulatory framework gives local manufacture and assembly of finished Chinese Proprietary Medicines (CPM) a formal place in the supply chain. HSA requires the relevant dealer licences and product listing, with manufacturing and assembly facilities subject to licensing and Good Manufacturing Practice requirements (HSA, 2026c, 2026d). The capsule example connects the retail shelf to this framework. Raw herbs, foods and soup packs fall under their respective product categories.',
48: 'Packaging gives buyers a named product and information about its contents and source. HSA’s CPM guidelines require full labelling in English, with corresponding information in any additional language. Labels must include product and ingredient information, batch and expiry details, and the relevant manufacturer, importer or assembler information (HSA, 2025a, section 5). These requirements shape how finished medicines are presented in Singapore. HSA treats product listing separately from an assessment of therapeutic efficacy (HSA, 2026b), a distinction relevant to reading health claims on the box.',
49: 'Liu et al.’s (2026) study of Chinese herbal medicines sold in Malaysia examines how legal requirements shape translated names and therapeutic statements. It offers a regional comparison for the work performed by packaging: carrying medical vocabulary into consumer communication. At Tong Keow, the photographed “Mind Soothe” box provides a concrete example of a product encountered through a name and short description. We interpret this as one way traditional vocabulary becomes accessible in a retail setting governed by Singapore’s requirements.',
50: 'Quality labels provide another way for the shop to communicate with buyers. Staff emphasised fresh colour and sulphur-free materials; our cabinet photograph shows a sulphur-free Dang Shen label and a separate American ginseng label (Figure 3). These visible claims invite attention to material quality and identity. We interpret them as part of how the shop presents its products as trustworthy. HSA’s guidance on supplement claims cautions against treating “natural” as a guarantee of safety (HSA, 2026a), keeping the discussion of trust distinct from a safety assessment.',
52: 'The staff member described Malay customers buying dessert ingredients and Indian customers buying nuts, alongside a largely Chinese clientele. This account gives the mixed food and medicinal range a cultural dimension. Customers have different reasons to enter the same shop, and ordinary food purchases bring the medicine hall into a wider range of shopping routines.',
53: 'We interpret these overlapping routines as everyday cultural contact. Dessert ingredients and nuts give customers a familiar reason to use a space that also sells medicinal products. The shop can become part of their everyday surroundings while allowing different understandings of its goods. In this account, cultural change takes place through the shared use of a neighbourhood shop. This gives a concrete form to the presentation’s discussion of cultural contact.',
56: 'The ginseng profiles clarify why individual assessment matters within clinical TCM. Table A1 describes ginseng as neutral in nature and American ginseng as cool and nourishing Yin. The staff’s fatigue–tonic pairing in Table A6 gives a customer a starting point for discussing a product. Clinical selection requires considering the person’s wider signs and symptoms, a process known as 辨证论治, or pattern differentiation and treatment (WHO, 2022). The contrast between the two profiles makes this requirement concrete: choosing a preparation involves more information than the symptom of fatigue alone.',
57: 'The retail setting also belongs within a wider picture of health care. MOH reported that nearly 40% of TCM users in the 2022 National Population Health Survey combined TCM and Western medicine for the same condition (Ministry of Health [MOH], 2025). This national finding places TCM within overlapping practices of care. It offers useful context for understanding a medicine-hall purchase as one possible part of a person’s health routine.',
59: 'The themes form a connected account of access. Food use gives herbs a household purpose; prepared packs reduce the effort of choosing and portioning them; packaging explains intended uses. Local branding and manufacture place some products within Singapore’s businesses and regulation. We interpret these features together as the practical means through which Tong Keow brings traditional materials into ordinary use.',
60: 'The mixed medicine-and-food shop gives these activities one location. Branded soup packs connect the Hougang outlet to the wider business, while the neighbourhood’s established residential population gives context to the clientele described by staff. These features help explain the fit between the shelf range and household routines. The shop’s everyday role emerges from the combination of goods, preparation formats and place.',
61: 'Unschuld’s (2018) account of adaptation and Yang’s (2022) history help frame this interpretation. Tong Keow adds a concrete retail setting to that discussion: continuity takes shape in soup displays, prepared goods and the ordinary activities of a neighbourhood shop.',
63: 'This report draws on one visit and one staff interview. Accounts of demand, customer preferences and cross-cultural purchases come from staff; sales records, customer interviews and branch comparisons would allow a fuller assessment. Neighbourhood statistics describe the surrounding population, and the promotional, journalistic and public-relations accounts serve as industry comparisons.',
64: 'Complete soup recipes and sourcing and purchasing records were unavailable. The pictured Nature’s Green capsules have a confirmed Singapore manufacturing link; their ingredient origins remain unknown, and the interview identified the product category rather than the brand. Physician presence was unclear, and we recorded no consultation, prescription or patient outcome. MOH (2026) provides the wider professional-registration context.',
67: 'Tong Keow makes TCM available through household food, prepared products and a shop that combines medicinal and everyday goods. The soup displays place herbs within cooking; the packs shift some preparation work to the seller; local branding and manufacture give products a Singapore retail form. Staff accounts connect these features to nourishment, convenience and customers’ routines.',
68: 'Our central finding is that adaptation gives traditional materials practical ways to remain in everyday use. Tong Keow’s contribution is visible in how products are selected, presented and fitted to household activities in Hougang. This retail role also gives clinical assessment a clear place: accessible products provide an entry point to TCM, while individual treatment requires attention to the person’s pattern of signs and symptoms.',
}

# Connect the course reading to the observed shop while keeping the narrative concise.
updates.update({
6: 'This revision connects the fieldwork to Lecture 5 through everyday care, geographical locality and cultural exchange. The seven survey records remain in Appendix A. Staff accounts, photographs and interpretations are distinguished, and industry sources retain their stated status. The argument explains how retail adaptations support everyday use, with individual clinical assessment discussed in Section 6.',
8: 'At Tong Keow’s Hougang outlet, herbs and packaged soups sit alongside grains and everyday foods. Staff described purchases for nourishment, throat comfort, fatigue and concerns about daily routines. These shelves and accounts place Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) within ordinary shopping and cooking, the starting point of our report.',
9: 'We argue that Tong Keow helps sustain TCM by fitting traditional materials into everyday household practices. Section 3 explains how food use gives herbs a familiar purpose. Section 4 examines how prepared products reduce the work of using them. Section 5 considers their fit with Singapore’s production, regulation and cultural setting. Section 6 brings these connections together and explains the role of individual assessment in treatment. Appendix A retains the detailed survey findings.',
12: 'We visited Tong Keow on 13 September 2026, photographed the displays and interviewed one staff member. This report draws on the transcripts, presentation script and photographs. We paraphrase clear statements where transcription errors occur. Staff accounts refer to this interview (Tong Keow staff member, personal communication, September 13, 2026).',
13: 'Staff descriptions refer to the interview; descriptions of displays refer to our photographs. We identify our interpretations when explaining their significance. Published sources and Lecture 5 provide context. Accounts of popular and lower-demand products represent the staff member’s retail experience.',
15: 'The presentation traces Tong Keow’s Toa Payoh shop to 1968, consistent with a directory’s account of its fiftieth anniversary in 2018. Business registration came later: Tong Keow Medicine and Provision Shop on 29 March 1975, and Tong Keow Medical Hall Pte. Ltd. on 1 January 2015. Both list 70 Lorong 4 Toa Payoh #01-333, Toa Payoh Vista. Their classifications cover Chinese medicine shops and provision or convenience stores (codes 47723 and 47102), matching the medicinal and food range observed (Accounting and Corporate Regulatory Authority [ACRA], n.d.; Healthcare.com.sg, n.d.; Singapore Department of Statistics [DOS], n.d.).',
16: 'The tutor described Tong Keow as a chain, and the directory links its Toa Payoh and Hougang outlets. Our photographs show branded soup packs alongside herbs, grains and other foods. The branding connects the branch to the wider business. We interpret the mixed range as central to its everyday role: herbal goods can be encountered during an ordinary food-shopping trip.',
18: 'Staff described customers as mainly Chinese, middle-aged and older, including people in their forties and fifties. Hougang planning area had 228,140 residents in June 2024. HDB separately reported 57,557 dwelling units and 165,450 residents in its Hougang flats at 31 March 2024. These totals cover different housing populations (DOS, 2024; Housing & Development Board [HDB], 2024, pp. 11–12).',
19: 'Hougang Central, the subzone around the outlet, had about 4,620 residents in June 2025. Official rounded age-band totals put around 1,070 residents at age 65 or above, roughly 23% (DOS, 2025). This substantial older population provides a local setting for the clientele described by staff and for interpreting the shop’s household foods and nourishment products.',
20: 'Chua et al.’s (2025) 647 Hougang respondents averaged 69 years of age and 22 years of residence. Seventeen per cent lived alone, compared with 15% of 552 Taman Jurong respondents; the difference was not statistically significant. The long residence is especially relevant here: it places the medicine hall within an established community where household shopping and care take place.',
21: 'Hougang’s Teochew history adds another dimension. The National Heritage Board records the community around Tou Mu Kung and its fishing, farming and rubber-growing activities (National Heritage Board [NHB], n.d.). We interpret this history and residential setting as context for the shop’s mixed medicinal and food range. Staff supplied the account of its current customers.',
24: 'Staff named Dang Shen, Huang Qi, Yu Zhu, Huai Shan, red dates and goji as common soup ingredients. The presentation included ginseng/American ginseng and monk fruit among its eight herb entries. Table A1 separates the ginseng profiles. Prepared packs and a family cooking image place these materials in the setting of a household meal (Figures 1–2).',
25: 'Soup gives herbs a familiar household purpose. Lecture 5 describes Chinese medicine as a cultural framework that enters everyday food and health habits ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., secs. 1, 35). This helps us interpret the display through nourishing life, 养生, and medicine and food sharing a source, 药食同源. Lim et al. (2005) identify health maintenance as a significant reason for use, while Yang (2022) situates medicine in changing community life. The fieldwork gives this idea a concrete form: herbal materials enter the household through cooking.',
27: 'The presentation grouped soups under five labels: Qing Bu Liang 清补凉, Dang Shen–Huang Qi soup 党参北芪补气汤, Yu Zhu–Huai Shan soup 玉竹淮山润燥汤, goji–red date soup 枸杞红枣养生汤 and Five Finger Fig soup 五指毛桃健脾汤. Table A4 records their named ingredients and connects familiar preparations to specific forms of care.',
28: 'Staff and the presentation associated these soups with digestion, moistening and light nourishment, 清补. Light replenishment offers an everyday approach alongside stronger tonification, 大补. Named packs make this vocabulary practical: buyers can take a preparation home to cook. The Spleen and Liver describe TCM functions and relationships (World Health Organization [WHO], 2022). We interpret the packs as bringing these ideas into meals.',
30: 'Staff linked monk fruit’s popularity and lighter soups to Singapore’s environment. Meteorological Service Singapore describes high temperatures, abundant rainfall and humidity throughout the year, with monsoon and inter-monsoon periods (Meteorological Service Singapore [MSS], n.d.). This gives climatic context to the presentation’s phrase “without four distinct seasons” and the staff account of local demand.',
31: 'Lecture 5’s concept of geographical locality connects environment, diet and constitution to medical interpretation ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., secs. 15, 24). The Suwen reading also relates seasonal habits to maintaining health (Unschuld & Tessenow, 2011, pp. 45–57). We interpret the staff’s explanation as this reasoning expressed in retail advice: Singapore’s weather becomes a reason to discuss lighter soups. The lecture’s attention to individual signs keeps this contextual link precise. The herb profiles likewise distinguish functions, including Yu Zhu’s moistening of dryness.',
34: 'Staff described younger customers as buying fewer raw herbs and preferring teas, supplements and simpler preparations. Table A2 retains eight presentation categories, including ginseng tonics, herbal teas, soup packs, mood-support products and medicated oils. This account directs attention to product form: staff associate easier preparation with younger customers’ routines.',
35: 'Soup packs make convenience visible. Ingredients are selected and portioned, so the household can begin with cooking. Staff explain their intended use, and buyers complete preparation at home. Capsules require less preparation, while the photographed multi-buy promotion gives soup packs a familiar retail offer. We interpret convenience as a redistribution of effort between seller and household, making traditional materials easier to choose and use.',
37: 'Industry comparisons describe deliberate changes in how herbal goods are offered. Nanyang Polytechnic discusses Heng Foh Tong’s herbal-drink redesign for millennials (Nanyang Polytechnic [NYP], n.d.). Amacha markets TCM-inspired milk teas; a founder interview reported over 150,000 cups sold in eight months (Amacha, n.d.; Angelynn T., 2026). Teck Soon’s owner reported a 30% sales increase after moving herbs from drawers to storefront displays in 1982 (Chin, 2024). These are promotional, journalistic or institutional public-relations accounts, not peer-reviewed studies. Their value lies in the specific strategies described: changing product forms and making herbs easier to encounter. They give context to Tong Keow’s prepared packs and staff account of convenience.',
39: 'Staff associated late nights, long work hours, stress and prolonged phone use with liver-care or mood-support products. The supplied photograph illustrates this category with Nature’s Green Mind Soothe Capsules. Its English name accompanies the TCM language of soothing Liver-related constraint, 疏肝解郁. We interpret this wording as a connection between therapeutic vocabulary and familiar concerns about daily life. “Liver” retains its TCM meaning.',
40: 'Official labour data sharpen this reading: average usual weekly working hours among employed residents fell from 44.2 a decade earlier to 41.4 in 2025 (Ministry of Manpower [MOM], 2026). The interview’s value lies in describing perceived strain in customers’ routines and how staff connect those concerns to products. National work-hour trends provide a separate context.',
41: 'Lower-demand examples further explain the appeal of simpler formats. The presentation listed Ban Zhi Lian 半枝莲, specialist herbs used in combinations and strong tonic formulas 大补方 (Table A5). Staff linked lower appeal to unfamiliarity and preparation effort; the presentation also discussed stronger tonics’ suitability in Singapore’s climate. Staff said poorly selling items were stocked in smaller quantities. Together, these accounts suggest that understanding and preparation matter to a product’s place on the shelf.',
44: 'Lecture 5 treats localisation as adaptation to climate, available materials and society ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., sec. 8). At Tong Keow, it appears at several production stages. Staff described imports mainly from China, citing variety and cost, with limited local cultivation. The presentation retained cactus 仙人掌, roselle 洛神花, patent medicines 中成药, Five Flower Tea 五花茶 and monk-fruit tea 罗汉果茶 (Table A3). The Health Sciences Authority (HSA) lists Tong Jum Chew as manufacturer of the pictured Nature’s Green Mind Soothe Capsules and Singapore as the manufacturing country (HSA, 2025b). Finished-medicine production provides a specific local link.',
45: 'Branded packs also give herbs a recognisable retail form. Staff described local processing and packing. Our interpretation centres on the visible selection and portioning of ingredients, named uses and local branding. Packaging actively shapes the product encountered by shoppers. Table A3 distinguishes cultivation, manufacture and packaging to clarify each example’s connection to Singapore.',
46: 'Local manufacture and assembly of finished Chinese Proprietary Medicines (CPM) occupy a formal place in Singapore’s supply chain. HSA requires relevant dealer licences and product listing, with facilities subject to licensing and Good Manufacturing Practice requirements (HSA, 2026c, 2026d). The capsule example connects the shelf to this framework. Raw herbs, foods and soup packs have their respective product categories.',
48: 'Packaging identifies a product’s contents and source. HSA’s CPM guidelines require full English labelling, corresponding information in additional languages, and product and ingredient details. Batch, expiry and relevant manufacturer, importer or assembler information are also required (HSA, 2025a, section 5). These requirements shape the box shoppers encounter. Product listing is separate from an assessment of therapeutic efficacy (HSA, 2026b), a distinction that matters when reading health claims.',
49: 'Liu et al. (2026) examine how legal requirements shape translated names and therapeutic statements on herbal medicines in Malaysia. Their regional comparison helps explain packaging’s role in carrying medical vocabulary into consumer communication. At Tong Keow, “Mind Soothe” gives this process a concrete form. We interpret the name and short description as a way to make traditional vocabulary accessible within Singapore’s regulatory setting.',
50: 'Quality labels offer another form of communication. Staff emphasised fresh colour and sulphur-free materials; our photograph shows a sulphur-free Dang Shen label and a separate American ginseng label (Figure 3). We interpret these claims about quality and identity as invitations to trust the shop’s goods. HSA cautions against treating “natural” as a safety guarantee (HSA, 2026a), a necessary limit on that reading.',
52: 'Staff described Malay customers buying dessert ingredients and Indian customers buying nuts, alongside a largely Chinese clientele. Ordinary food purchases offer different groups familiar reasons to enter the same medicine hall. Eu Yan Sang’s halal product category provides an industry comparison for addressing different consumers’ requirements (Eu Yan Sang, n.d.). This commercial source illustrates another route to access; the staff account at Tong Keow concerns food shopping.',
53: 'Lecture 5 discusses how the same herb can acquire different meanings across Chinese and Malay medicine ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., secs. 35, 40, 46). This helps us interpret Tong Keow’s shared shopping space. Food goods can make the hall familiar while customers retain different understandings of its products. The fieldwork adds a retail perspective to the lecture: cultural contact can take place through overlapping routines and uses. Here, the staff account concerns everyday purchases; historical therapeutic exchange provides the comparison.',
56: 'The ginseng profiles clarify the importance of individual assessment. Table A1 describes ginseng as neutral and American ginseng as cool and nourishing Yin. Staff’s fatigue–tonic pairing in Table A6 gives a starting point for discussion. Clinical selection considers wider signs and symptoms through 辨证论治, or pattern differentiation and treatment (WHO, 2022). Lecture 5 similarly contrasts treatment approaches for cold-damage and warm-disease patterns ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., sec. 20). The ginseng comparison makes this principle tangible: the choice depends on the person’s fuller presentation.',
57: 'Lecture 5 traces TCM’s changing position from major community healing system to complementary care ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., secs. 4, 12). MOH reported that nearly 40% of TCM users in the 2022 National Population Health Survey combined TCM and Western medicine for the same condition (Ministry of Health [MOH], 2025). This places TCM within overlapping practices of care and gives context to a medicine-hall purchase as one part of a health routine.',
59: 'The themes form a connected account of access. Food gives herbs a household purpose, packs reduce selection and preparation effort, and packaging communicates uses. Local branding and manufacture connect products to Singapore’s retail and regulatory setting. We interpret these features as practical means of bringing traditional materials into everyday use.',
60: 'The mixed shop gives these activities one location. Branding connects the branch to a wider business, while Hougang’s residential population gives context to the clientele described by staff. These features help explain the fit between its shelf range and household routines. The shop’s everyday role emerges through products, preparation and place.',
61: 'Lecture 5 makes adaptation central to Singapore’s medical history ("A Brief Introduction," n.d., sec. 47), alongside Unschuld’s (2018) account and Yang’s (2022) history. Our fieldwork extends this discussion into retail: ingredients gain everyday uses through familiar foods and prepared goods. The historical argument becomes visible at the scale of a neighbourhood shop.',
63: 'The report draws on one visit and one staff interview. Accounts of demand, preferences and cross-cultural purchases come from staff. Sales records, customer interviews and branch comparisons would deepen the assessment. Neighbourhood statistics describe the surrounding population, and industry accounts supply comparisons.',
64: 'Complete soup recipes and sourcing and purchasing records were unavailable. The pictured Nature’s Green capsules have a confirmed Singapore manufacturing link; ingredient origins remain unknown, and staff named the category rather than the brand. Physician presence was unclear, and we recorded no consultation, prescription or patient outcome. MOH (2026) supplies the wider professional-registration context.',
67: 'Tong Keow makes TCM available through food, prepared products and a mixed medicine hall. Soup displays place herbs within cooking, packs shift preparation work to the seller, and branding and manufacture give some products a Singapore retail form. Staff link this range to nourishment, convenience and customers’ routines.',
68: 'Our interpretation is that adaptation gives traditional materials practical ways to remain in everyday use. Tong Keow makes the lecture’s account of localisation visible in products fitted to Hougang household activities. Retail access introduces people to TCM, while individual treatment calls for attention to the person’s pattern of signs and symptoms.',
})

def rewrite(p, value):
    size = p.runs[0].font.size if p.runs else None
    p.clear()
    for part in re.findall(r'[\u3400-\u9fff]+|[^\u3400-\u9fff]+', value):
        r = p.add_run(part)
        if size: r.font.size = size
        if re.search(r'[\u3400-\u9fff]', part):
            face = 'Microsoft YaHei' if part == '辨证论治' else 'SimSun'
            r.font.name = face
            r._r.get_or_add_rPr().get_or_add_rFonts().set(qn('w:eastAsia'), face)

for i, value in updates.items():
    assert d.paragraphs[i].style.name == 'Normal', (i, d.paragraphs[i].text)
    rewrite(d.paragraphs[i], value)

reference_heading = next(p for p in d.paragraphs if p.text == 'References')
reference_element = OxmlElement('w:p')
reference_heading._p.addnext(reference_element)
lecture_reference = Paragraph(reference_element, reference_heading._parent)
first_reference = next(
    p for p in d.paragraphs if p.text.startswith('Accounting and Corporate Regulatory Authority.'))
if first_reference._p.pPr is not None:
    from copy import deepcopy
    lecture_reference._p.insert(0, deepcopy(first_reference._p.pPr))
lecture_reference.add_run('A brief introduction to Chinese medicine in Singapore.').italic = True
lecture_reference.add_run(' (n.d.). [GEC1044 Lecture 5 note summary supplied by the user; author and lecture date unspecified].')

d.save(output)
check = Document(output)
original = Document(source)
pics = lambda doc: [hashlib.sha256(s._inline.xml.encode()).hexdigest() for s in doc.inline_shapes]
assert pics(check) == pics(original)
